  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic cross-section of Earth’s mantle linking mantle structure, subduction, deep reservoirs, and mineral phase changes. A subducting lithospheric slab sinks from the surface into the mantle, while the continental crust sits above. The mantle is divided into the upper mantle, transition zone, and lower mantle, with depth markers near 410 km, 660 km, and the core–mantle boundary at about 2,891 km.\n\nThe image emphasizes how material circulates through the mantle. Slabs descend, and hot mantle plumes rise from deep near the core–mantle boundary toward the surface. Near the base of the mantle, two important deep structures are highlighted: a broad LLSVP and a thinner ULVZ layer. These seem to act as zones where deep material accumulates or from which plumes originate.\n\nColored symbols and numbered circles indicate different mineralogical transitions expected at different depths and settings, including changes in bridgmanite, ferropericlase, stishovite, CaSiO3 perovskite, NAL phase, and post-perovskite. The grouped labels suggest that specific phase transitions are associated with subducting slabs, plume source regions, and deep mantle heterogeneity. Overall, the figure shows the mantle as a dynamic system where convection and phase changes are tightly connected.",
